WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.042
ROGER MARTINEZ: One of
the most powerful features

00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:05.100
of Google Cloud Compute Engine
is managed instance groups.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.350
So managed instance groups
let you automatically scale

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.330
your application out to
handle those peak loads

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.180
and automatically heal in case
one of your instances go down.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.970
Today, I'm going to do a quick
dive into managed instance

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.520
groups and show you how
easy it is to set one up

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.780
for your application.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.450
On Google Cloud,
an instance group

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.430
is simply a group
of virtual machines

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.440
that can be managed
as a single entity.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.710
A managed instance group
is an instance group

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.950
of identical VMs created
based on an identical instance

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.598
template.

00:00:36.598 --> 00:00:38.640
A managed instance group
introduces a whole bunch

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.770
of functionality,
like autoscaling

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.470
for increasing the size of your
group depending on the load,

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.390
autohealing for
automatically replacing

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.390
unhealthy instances, and
multizone deployments

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.280
for higher availability.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.080
Before I get into
any of that, let's

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.300
review the four
Google Cloud resources

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.490
that are associated with
creating a managed instance

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:57.000
group.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.790
First is the instance
group manager.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.030
This represents the managed
instance group itself.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.400
The configuration of a
managed instance group manager

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.233
will include whether
the instance group will

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.360
be zonal or regional
and, if regional,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.130
which zones to distribute
the instances across.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.760
It'll also include the number
of instances in the group

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.770
and specify the second
resource that we

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.230
need, the instance template.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.700
The instance template is
essentially the cookie cutter

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.700
that the managed instance
group manager will use

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.290
to provision VMs in the group.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.755
The instance
template will specify

00:01:26.755 --> 00:01:28.380
much of the same
configuration that you

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.660
might include in a standalone
instance, like machine

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.020
size, disk, networking
settings, and more.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.210
You'll only need one
template, but you

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.730
do have the option of
specifying multiple ones so you

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.500
can do things like
roll out canary builds

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.740
of your applications to only a
subset of your instance group.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.870
Next we have a health check.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.880
A health check is
only required if you

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.400
want to enable autohealing
on your instance group.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.170
That's when instances
are automatically

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.200
replaced when they repeatedly
fail the health check.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.910
These health checks
are the same ones

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.740
a load balancer would
use to direct traffic

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.450
to healthy instances.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.010
For managed instance
groups, it's

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.720
used to decide whether
the instance needs

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.220
to be reprovisioned.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.720
When creating a
health check, you

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.580
can choose from five
different protocols,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.110
and you can specify
how rigorous you

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.720
want the health check to be by
adjusting the check interval

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:13.740
and thresholds.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.420
If you plan on
using autoscaling,

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.390
the last thing you'll need to
have is an autoscaling policy.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.670
The autoscaling policy
has all the configuration

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.790
that determines under what
circumstances new instances

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.220
will be added to and
removed from the group.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.310
It isn't required for a
managed instance group,

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.210
and it can be enabled and
disabled after being added.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.430
Let's take a quick look
at how we can set up

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.920
all of these resources and
have a managed instance group

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.520
up and running in no time.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.450
In this demonstration, I'll
be working from a new project

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.310
with no existing resources.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:47.120
And I'll have only the Compute
Admin IAM role assigned.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:48.680
In the Google Cloud
console, you'll

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.540
find an instance group
under Compute Engine

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.330
in the Navigation menu.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.830
You can create everything you
need for your managed instance

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:58.883
group in one shot by clicking
Create Instance Group.

00:02:58.883 --> 00:03:00.800
After giving it a snazzy
name, the first thing

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.290
you'll want to do is choose
from these three choices--

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:04.880
stateless managed
instance group,

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.480
stateful managed instance group,
or unmanaged instance group.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.250
Stateful and stateless managed
instance groups are similar,

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.760
except stateful will
let you preserve disks.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.220
Let's choose a stateless
managed instance group.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.310
The next big choice you
have to make is location.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.500
A zonal managed instance
group will provision VMs

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.660
in one single zone and is
fine for most use cases.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.630
But remember that a
regional managed instance

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:28.130
group will improve
your availability

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:30.770
by spreading your instances
across several zones.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:32.780
When you choose
multiple zones, it'll

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.700
let you specify which
zones in that region

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.100
you want to distribute
your instances across.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.920
Choose at least three to
maximize availability.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.250
Next, let's specify
an instance template.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:47.470
If the project doesn't have one,
we can create it right here.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:50.080
For this example, I'll just
create a simple template

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.980
that will create an E2 medium
with a Debian boot disk.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.570
Next, let's add an autoscaling
policy in this section here.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.310
For this demonstration,
I'll leave it

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.810
with the default values.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.930
I'll dive deeper into all these
settings, like cooldown period

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.360
and scaling controls,
in a later video.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.030
By default, it's going
to use CPU utilization

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.060
at 60% as a metric that
it uses to scale up.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.770
But you can adjust
that percentage

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.779
or replace the metric
with load balancer

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:20.950
utilization or a Stackdriver
monitoring metric.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.140
If you want, you can also
add additional metrics

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.360
by hitting Add New Metric.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.698
There's no one-size-fits-all
configuration here.

00:04:27.698 --> 00:04:29.740
It's going to depend on
your application's needs,

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:33.865
but the default values will
work for most use cases.

00:04:33.865 --> 00:04:35.740
One thing I will change
is the minimum number

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.570
of instances, because
remember, this

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:38.860
is a regional instance group.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.277
So I want to make
sure that I have

00:04:40.277 --> 00:04:42.940
at least one instance in each
of the zones that I specified.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:45.400
Down here at the bottom is
where we configure autohealing

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.470
by specifying a health check.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.050
I don't have one yet, so
I'll just create a new one.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:51.913
The default here is TCP.

00:04:51.913 --> 00:04:53.830
If your application needs
something different,

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:57.100
you have the options of these
other protocols in this list.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:00.280
I'll select HTTP and specify
the request path for the health

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:02.800
page of my web application.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.720
Down here under
Health Criteria is

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.690
where we indicate how frequently
the health check should run

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:09.580
and at what point to
consider the state

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.970
of an individual
instance as unhealthy.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:14.950
With these default
values, the health check

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:18.213
will run against each
instance every 10 seconds.

00:05:18.213 --> 00:05:19.630
If it doesn't
receive the response

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:23.680
it's looking for in 5 seconds,
it'll count that as a failure.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.410
Three consecutive failures means
the health check state will

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.230
be unhealthy and will
only become healthy again

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.360
after two consecutive successes.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.060
Once we're done here, we're
ready to create our managed

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:36.820
instance group with
autoscaling and autohealing.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:40.120
You can click Create
down here or Command Line

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.330
to see the corresponding
gcloud commands.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:46.180
You can see here the four
resources we're created--

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:49.980
the instance template,
the health check,

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.590
the instance group
itself, and finally,

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:55.738
the autoscaling policy.

00:05:55.738 --> 00:05:57.780
That's a really quick
rundown, but there's plenty

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.280
more you can do to get the most
out of your managed instance

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:00.930
groups.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.190
For example, enabling
predictive autoscaling

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:05.190
will feed your instance
groups historical trends

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.230
and current load into
machine learning models

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:10.620
so that the autoscaler can
anticipate autoscale events.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.650
This way, the autoscaler
will behave more proactively

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.900
and have machines ready to
roll with less time waiting

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.400
for machines to initialize.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.890
For more information on
managed instance groups and all

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.630
they have to offer,
check out the link

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.850
to the documentation below.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:26.550
In the next video, I'm going
to discuss in more detail how

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.925
you can make the most out of
your managed instance groups

00:06:28.925 --> 00:06:31.680
by making sure you have an
effective autoscaling policy.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:32.430
See you then.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

